===Etymology 2===
Shortened from {{m|en|bebop}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|music genre}} A [[style]] of [[improvised]] [[jazz]] from the 1940s.
#* {{RQ:Kerouac On the Road|chapter=13|passage=That grand wild sound of '''bop''' floated from beer parlors; it mixed medleys with every kind of cowboy and boogie-woogie in the American night.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|Oxbridge slang}} A [[party]] hosted by a [[college]]'s [[JCR]] or [[MCR]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Johnny Rich|title=Push Guide to Which University|page=472|passage=Theatres; Music House used for bands; May Ball; very popular weekly '''bops''' in JCR and MCR; library (57,000 books); 40 networked PCs, 24-hrs.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Owen Jones|title=Chavs: The Demonization of the Working Class|page=120|passage=At universities like Oxford, middle-class students hold 'chav '''bops'''' where they dress up as this working-class caricature.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|countable}} A good [[song]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[dance]] to this [[music]], or any sort of popular music with a strong beat.
# {{lb|en|slang|intransitive}} To [[have sex]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Tim Cockey|title=The Hearse You Came In On|passage=I'm here because your wife and I have been '''bopping''' like bunnies. Here are the pictures to prove it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|first=Terrence Oral|last=Taylor|title=Dancing with the Boogie Man|page=196|passage={{...}} You aren't the Boy Scout you pretend to be. I'll bet you two '''bopped''' all night long.” Colin shook his head. “You're wrong. We didn't.” “You just took her home?” “That's about it.”}}

